1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 c5 7. d5 {White takes the game into a Benoni , 7.dxc5 dxc5 8.Qxd8 Rxd8 9.Bxc5 Nc6 has be shown to be fine for Black with his dark squared control after moves like ..Nd7 and ..Nd4} a6 8. a4 e6 9. Qd2 exd5 10. cxd5 Re8 11. Nge2 Nbd7 {Only now, Black does not want to allow Nh3-f2 when the knight is well placed to support the e4 pawn and thus allow f4.} 12. Ng3 h5 13. Be2 h4 14. Nf1 h3 $2 {The source of Black's later problems. The pawn is weak, the Nf1 hops back into the game and White's kingside pawns are released.} 15. g4 Ne5 16. Ng3 Bd7 17. O-O $1 {White surmises that his kingside play will outweight Black's queenside majority and rejects the 'normal' move 17.a5 when Black goes b5 andaxb6 gives him play down the b file} b5 18. g5 Nh7 19. f4 b4 (19... Nc4 20. Bxc4 bxc4 21. f5 (21. e5 dxe5 22. f5)) 20. Nd1 Ng4 21. Kh1 Nxe3 22. Nxe3 {When a white knight gets to c4 unchallenged in the Benoni the resulting pressure on d6 & e5 is laways unpleasant for Black. White was just going to play Nc4 and then build for e5/f5 but maybe 22..a5 23.Nc4 Bc8 and ..Ba6} Nxg5 {Did Hebden underestimate his young opponent or was he desperate ?} 23. fxg5 Qxg5 24. Bc4 Bd4 25. Rae1 Bxa4 26. Qf2 Ra7 27. b3 Bb5 28. Qf3 {Although material is level, three pawns are usually equivalent to a piece White has a big attack and the Black pawns are blockaded.} Bxe3 29. Rxe3 Bxc4 30. bxc4 Re5 (30... Ree7 31. Nf5) 31. Qf2 a5 32. Rf3 Ree7 33. Rg1 {White has a winning attack} Red7 34. Nf5 {} Qh5 35. Rf4 Qh8 36. Rxg6+ Kf8 37. Rh6 Qa1+ 38. Qg1 Qxg1+ 39. Kxg1 f6 40. Rh8+ {Source: CHESS, March 1995, Vol.59/12, p45 (with more detailed notes by Pein)} 1-0

